# Netlify redirects for the VeriHire client.
#
# These live in `client/public/` rather than in `netlify.toml` on purpose: everything in
# public/ is copied verbatim into dist/, so the rules travel with the build artifact and
# apply identically whether the deploy came from a CI git build or a direct upload of the
# folder. Redirects in netlify.toml are resolved relative to the repo, which makes them
# silently absent from a `--dir` deploy.
#
# ★ Order matters — Netlify takes the FIRST match. The API rules must precede the SPA
# fallback, or /api/v1/jobs would be answered with index.html and a 200, and axios would
# try to parse a web page as JSON.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- API proxy
#
# Uncomment and set the real origin once the Express API is deployed.
#
# This has to be a proxy rather than pointing the client at the API's own domain via
# VITE_API_URL. The refresh token is an httpOnly SameSite=Strict cookie, and Strict means
# the browser will not attach it to a cross-site request — so a cross-origin API would
# authenticate on login and then fail every token refresh, logging users out the moment
# the 15-minute access token expired. Proxying keeps the browser on a single origin, which
# is the arrangement the cookie flags and the vite dev proxy were both written for.
#
# /api/*  https://REPLACE-WITH-API-HOST/api/:splat  200!

# Until that origin exists, fail loudly and in the envelope shape the client already
# parses. Without this line the SPA fallback below would answer API calls with HTML and a
# 200, which surfaces as a TypeError deep inside a component instead of an error state.
/api/*  /api-unavailable.json  503!

# ------------------------------------------------------------- SPA fallback
#
# Deep links like /jobs/abc123 are client-side routes with no file on disk. 200 rather
# than 301 so the URL the user typed stays the URL in the address bar.
/*  /index.html  200
